# Installer userspace: everything needed to INSTALL a machine, and nothing
# needed to BE one.
#
# This is deliberately not a variant of base-system. An installer has a
# different job and a different cost centre. It must be able to partition,
# make filesystems, fetch and verify a package index, configure a network
# and run the canonical installer tooling against a fresh root. It does not
# need to be a working server, and it must not carry a kernel, an init
# system or the kernel's prepared development tree merely to do its job.
#
# The bootable installer payload is this profile PLUS a kernel PLUS an
# initramfs. Those are joined later, once saphira-initramfs-tools exists;
# until then the payload is userspace only, and pretending otherwise would
# put a kernel in every test of the installation path.
#
# Intentions ONLY: the package manager owns dependency closure. Never
# enumerate resolved dependencies here; prove the closure with the
# repository index instead (see saphira-profiles(5)).

# The installer runtime, and the layout it installs into.
musl
saphira-base-abi
saphira-baselayout
bash
coreutils
# The installer tooling is POSIX shell and it uses find(1) and awk. These are
# not incidental conveniences: saphira-install refuses a non-empty target with
# find, and saphira-bootstrap builds its package list with awk, so without
# them the installer cannot run at all. mawk rather than gawk, per the clean-
# root policy recorded in the mawk recipe.
findutils
mawk

# Storage: partition tables, filesystem creation, discovery, mounting.
# Only the ext4/direct tooling ships here: XFS/LVM/ZFS creation tools
# are apk-added by the menu per manifest (the installer has network by
# requirement), so the payload stays small and clean-image guards keep
# holding (the installer installs ZFS targets without BEING one).
util-linux
e2fsprogs
dosfstools

# The package manager. The central operation of an install is
#   apk ... --root <new-root> ...
# against the canonical repository configured in /etc/apk/repositories.
apk-tools

# Fetching and verifying that repository. Repository policy belongs to APK
# configuration, never to the installer: /etc/apk/repositories is the single
# source of truth, and no tool here may substitute a path of its own.
openssl
ca-certificates
wget
zlib

# Networking: the installer has to be able to configure an interface and
# resolve a name in order to be usable over SSH.
iproute2
iputils
dhcpcd

# Remote installation is the point of many installs.
# openssh is ONE package, carrying both the client and sshd; there is no
# openssh-server.
openssh
# vim, because this is Saphira.
vim

# Bootloader installation happens AFTER the destination is populated, from
# the installer, for BIOS and UEFI alike.
grub
efibootmgr

# The v0 guided menu and the engine it drives. dialog is the text UI
# backend (serial-safe); saphira-image-creator owns partitioning,
# filesystems and bootloaders, so the menu shells out to it instead of
# reimplementing disk writing.
dialog
saphira-image-creator

# The canonical installation tooling itself. It is what turns a profile and
# an empty directory into a coherent system, so the installer carries the
# same tools it will use on its target rather than a reimplementation.
saphira-install-tools
